        <section id="dc-faqs" class="dc-section">
            <h2>Dash Cam FAQs</h2>

            <h3>What is the best dash cam in this guide?</h3>
            <p>
                For premium front and rear performance, the VIOFO A229 Pro is the strongest overall pick in this guide because it combines 4K front, 2K rear, dual STARVIS 2 sensors, HDR, GPS, and 5GHz WiFi in one higher-end package. For overall value, the ROVE R2-4K DUAL is the easier recommendation. :contentReference[oaicite:67]{index=67} :contentReference[oaicite:68]{index=68}
            </p>

            <h3>Is a front and rear dash cam better than a front-only dash cam?</h3>
            <p>
                For most drivers, yes. A front and rear dash cam covers more accident scenarios, tail-end impacts, and parking incidents than a front-only camera. Front-only models still make sense if you want a very small dash cam, a cleaner windshield, or a lower total cost. :contentReference[oaicite:69]{index=69} :contentReference[oaicite:70]{index=70}
            </p>

            <h3>What is the best dash cam for Uber drivers?</h3>
            <p>
                A 3 channel dash cam is usually the best choice for Uber drivers because it records front, interior, and rear footage. In this guide, the Vantrue N4S is the strongest overall rideshare recommendation, while the REDTIGER F17 is the better value option. :contentReference[oaicite:71]{index=71}
            </p>

            <h3>Do I need GPS on a dash cam?</h3>
            <p>
                Not always, but GPS is highly useful if you want location, speed, and route information tied to your footage. Many of the stronger picks in this guide include built-in GPS, including the ROVE R2-4K DUAL, REDTIGER F7NP, VIOFO A229 Pro, VIOFO A229 Plus, 70mai A810S, Vantrue E1 Pro, and BOTSLAB 4 Channel. :contentReference[oaicite:72]{index=72} :contentReference[oaicite:73]{index=73} :contentReference[oaicite:74]{index=74}
            </p>

            <h3>What matters more: 4K, parking mode, or night vision?</h3>
            <p>
                For many buyers, parking mode and night vision are more important than 4K alone. A higher-resolution camera is helpful, but a well-tuned sensor, better HDR, stronger low-light performance, and useful parking protection can matter more in real-world incidents. That is why some non-4K or balanced-resolution models in this guide still rank very highly. :contentReference[oaicite:75]{index=75} :contentReference[oaicite:76]{index=76}
            </p>

            <h3>Are mini dash cams worth buying?</h3>
            <p>
                Yes, especially if you want a hidden installation or dislike bulky screens on the windshield. The Garmin Dash Cam Mini 3 is the best simple compact choice in this guide, while the Vantrue E1 Pro and VIOFO A119M Pro offer stronger feature sets in compact bodies. :contentReference[oaicite:77]{index=77} :contentReference[oaicite:78]{index=78} :contentReference[oaicite:79]{index=79}
            </p>
